*======================================================================
* kdefs.a99 - the kernel's definitions: hardware addresses, the memory
* map (CPU, scratchpad, slot A, SAMS pages, VRAM), key codes, colour
* roles, screen geometry, file-service constants.
*
* Every kernel source, the loader and every module include this file or
* its export, kernel.equ (a module cannot COPY kdefs.a99: the kernel is
* assembled first and its symbols, these included, are written to
* build/kernel.equ).  The application's own numbers (its name, how many
* SAMS pages it needs, the page of each of its modules) come from
* appcfg.a99, which tools/lkbuild.py writes from app.json.
*
* Lantern Kernel 1.0.  MIT License, (c) 2026 Rebecca Sutter.
*======================================================================

       COPY "appcfg.a99"
       COPY "macros.a99"

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Hardware
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* VDP ports
VDPRD  EQU  >8800            VRAM read data
VDPSTA EQU  >8802            VDP status register
VDPWD  EQU  >8C00            VRAM write data
VDPWA  EQU  >8C02            VRAM / VDP register address

* GROM ports (the loader lifts the console font for its refusal screens)
GRMRD  EQU  >9800            GROM read data (auto-increments)
GRMWA  EQU  >9C02            GROM address, high byte first

* Sound
SOUND  EQU  >8400            TMS9919 sound chip

* AMS / SAMS mapper
AMSCRU EQU  >1E00            CRU base: bit 0 = registers visible, bit 1 = mapping on
AMSREG EQU  >4000            mapper registers, one word per 4K window: >4000 + 2*window

* Cartridge bank select (write any value to >6000 + 2*bank)
CBANK0 EQU  >6000            cart bank 0 select; bank n at >6000 + 2n

* The console's interrupt routine (>0900, workspace >83C0) runs only inside
* VSYNC, where interrupts are briefly allowed.  It advances the frame
* counter, plays sound lists, and blanks the screen when its timer wraps
* (about nine minutes at 60 Hz).  The kernel clears that timer on every key,
* so the screen blanks after nine idle minutes, as the TI's own programs do.
ISRTMR EQU  >8379            frame counter kept by the console ISR
ISRFLG EQU  >83C2            >50 = no sprite motion, no QUIT key
SNDPTC EQU  >83CC            sound list pointer
SNDDUR EQU  >83CE            sound list duration counter (0 = finished)
TMRINC EQU  >83FC            the console ISR adds this byte (R14's high byte in the GPL workspace) to >8379
*                            every frame: VSYNC needs it non-zero, and a DSR may disturb it
SNDFLG EQU  >83FD            bit 0 set = the sound list is in VDP RAM
VR1CPY EQU  >83D4            console's copy of VDP register 1 (written back when it blanks)
SCRTMO EQU  >83D6            console screen-blank timer (+2 a frame; blanks at 0)
GPLWS  EQU  >83E0            the GPL workspace: DSRs run in it

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* SAMS pages (4K each; identity-mapped at boot, so page n of the fixed
* windows sits at n<<12).  The kernel owns pages >00-1F's fixed roles;
* from PGDATA up the build assigns the application's pages (appcfg.a99).
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
PGLOWA EQU  >02              slot A (>2000, >3000): state and the stack
PGKERN EQU  >0A              resident kernel (>A000, >B000)
PGSLTC EQU  >0C              slot C's default pair (>C000, >D000): modules run here
PGLOWB EQU  >0E              slot B's default pair (>E000, >F000): data is paged here
PGDATA EQU  >10              data bank: glyphs, colour schemes (2 pages)
PGMOD0 EQU  >12              the first page the build gives a module
* Pages >04-09 are free for the application (the CPU's windows >4000-9FFF are
* not RAM, so these pages exist only when mapped into slot B or C).  The
* loader's memory test starts at PGDATA, so they are not tested at boot.
* Pages >00-01 are the console's windows and are never used.

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Scratchpad (>8300 - >83FF).  R0-R15 live in the workspace, so the
* console's own variables sit among ours.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
WS     EQU  >8300            the one workspace: R0-R15 (LWPI WS)
HOT    EQU  >8340            kernel hot variables
FRAME  EQU  HOT+0            16-bit frame counter (VSYNC and the idle polls add 1)
CURPA  EQU  HOT+2            pages mapped in slot A (>2000,>3000), (first<<8)|second
CURPB  EQU  HOT+4            pages mapped in slot B (>E000,>F000)
CURPC  EQU  HOT+6            pages mapped in slot C (>C000,>D000)
F18FLG EQU  HOT+8            >0001 if an F18A answered (the loader refuses otherwise)
F18VER EQU  HOT+10           F18A firmware version (status register 14), e.g. >0019
NPAGES EQU  HOT+12           SAMS pages present: 32, 64, 128 or 256 (256 = 1M or more)
QUITF  EQU  HOT+14           non-zero: the application asks to quit to the TI title
VDPPIC EQU  >8350            non-zero: the F18A is a PICO9918 (DSR calls keep this word)
SCRTCH EQU  >8380            64 bytes: the loader's relocated bank copier (free afterwards)

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Slot A (>2000 - >3FFF, pages >02-03): the kernel's state first, then
* the application's.  Nothing remaps slot A.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
KEYMAP EQU  >2000            6 bytes: keyboard columns 0-5 (0 bit = pressed)
KLAST  EQU  >2006            last chord code produced (word, code in the high byte), for auto-repeat
KHOLD  EQU  >2008            frames the current chord has been held
KMODS  EQU  >200A            modifiers of the last code: >80 FCTN, >40 SHIFT, >20 CTRL, >10 ALPHA
ALOCK  EQU  >200C            >0001 while ALPHA LOCK is down
KHEAD  EQU  >200E            key buffer: next slot to write (0-63)
KTAIL  EQU  >2010            key buffer: next slot to read (0-63)
KCOUNT EQU  >2012            codes taken from the buffer since boot (for tests)
KCURK  EQU  >2014            the key KDECOD found (column * 8 + row), or -1
KLKEY  EQU  >2016            the key that produced KLAST, or -1
KBUF   EQU  >2040            64-byte key buffer (codes, one byte each)
SCHEME EQU  >2080            current colour scheme (0-4)
TXTBUF EQU  >2090            16 bytes: number formatting and the hex routines
MCENT  EQU  >20A0            MODCALL: the entry
MCRET  EQU  >20A2            MODCALL: R0 from the call
NEXTPG EQU  >20A4            GOMOD: the module to switch to (first page), 0 = none
GOARG  EQU  >20A6            GOMOD: the argument handed to its INIT
PREVPG EQU  >20A8            the module that was running before the last switch (0 at boot)
GPUBUF EQU  >20B0            8 bytes: the GPU mailbox as built (VSHIFT)
NODMA  EQU  >20B8            non-zero: the F18A has no DMA engine (T80INI's test)
VMNUM  EQU  >20E8            the video mode set by VMODE (0-4)
VMOPT  EQU  >20EA            its options
TXCOLS EQU  >20EC            columns of the text screen (80, 40 or 32)
TXROWS EQU  >20EE            rows (24 or 30)
TXCELLS EQU >20F0            columns x rows
TXATTR EQU  >20F2            non-zero: this mode has an attribute table the text routines write
NAMBAS EQU  >20F4            the name table's VRAM address (NAMTAB, or NAMGM2 in Graphics II)
POOLBM EQU  >20C0            32 bytes: page-pool bitmap, a bit per page (1 = free)
POOLLO EQU  >20E0            pool: the first page it manages
POOLHI EQU  >20E2            pool: the page after its last
FREECT EQU  >20E4            pool: free pages

* File service (files.a99): >2100->21FF
FHOT   EQU  >2100            96 bytes: scratchpad >8320->837F while a DSR runs
FNAMB  EQU  >2160            8 bytes: the device name for the DSR search
FSR10  EQU  >2168            R10 across the DSR
FDCRU  EQU  >216A            CRU base of the ROM that answered
FDENT  EQU  >216C            what DLGPL calls
FD54   EQU  >216E            >8354, >8356, >83D0, >83D2 across the power-up
FD56   EQU  >2170
FDD0   EQU  >2172
FDD2   EQU  >2174
FDPWR  EQU  >2176            non-zero once the controller's power-up has run
FD70   EQU  >2178            >8370 as the controller's power-up left it
FERR   EQU  >217A            the last file operation's result
FLEN   EQU  >217C            the last record's length
FNMP   EQU  >217E            the name in use (address of a length-prefixed string)
FPAB   EQU  >2180            64 bytes: the PAB as built
*                            >21C0->21FF: free for the kernel's growth

MODRAM EQU  >2200            256 bytes of scratch for the running module (not kept across a module switch)
APPRAM EQU  >2300            the application's RAM: >2300 up to APPTOP
APPTOP EQU  >3F00            ... and the software stack above it
STKTOP EQU  >4000            R10 starts here: the first push writes >3FFE (256 bytes: 128 words)

* The data bank (pages PGDATA, mapped at >E000 by LDSCHM and T80INI)
DGLYPH EQU  >E000            256 glyphs x 8 bytes
DSCHEM EQU  >E800            5 colour schemes x 16 palette words (>0RGB)

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Key codes (a code is a byte; KEYGET returns it in the high byte)
*   >20->7E  printable characters
*   >0D      ENTER (with any modifier but CTRL)
*   >80->BF  CTRL + key: >80 | (key's unshifted code AND >3F)
*            (CTRL A = >81 ... CTRL Z = >9A, CTRL 0 = >B0 ... CTRL 9 = >B9)
*   >C0->FF  FCTN + key with no character: >C0 | (unshifted code AND >3F)
*            (FCTN E = >C5, FCTN S = >D3, FCTN D = >C4, FCTN X = >D8,
*             FCTN 1 = >F1 ... FCTN 9 = >F9, FCTN 0 = >F0, FCTN = = >FD)
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
KUP    EQU  >C5              FCTN E (up arrow)
KLEFT  EQU  >D3              FCTN S (left arrow)
KRIGHT EQU  >C4              FCTN D (right arrow)
KDOWN  EQU  >D8              FCTN X (down arrow)
KDEL   EQU  >F1              FCTN 1 (DEL)
KINS   EQU  >F2              FCTN 2 (INS)
KERASE EQU  >F3              FCTN 3 (ERASE)
KCLEAR EQU  >F4              FCTN 4 (CLEAR)
KBEGIN EQU  >F5              FCTN 5 (BEGIN)
KPROC  EQU  >F6              FCTN 6 (PROC'D)
KAID   EQU  >F7              FCTN 7 (AID)
KREDO  EQU  >F8              FCTN 8 (REDO)
KBACK  EQU  >F9              FCTN 9 (BACK)
KQUIT  EQU  >FD              FCTN = (QUIT)
KENTER EQU  >0D              ENTER

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Colour roles: palette entries 1-15.  A position-attribute byte is
* (foreground role << 4) | background role; a colour scheme is a palette
* of 16 words, so changing scheme recolours the whole screen without
* rewriting a cell.  Entry 0 is transparent (the backdrop shows).
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
RBG    EQU  1                page background
RTEXT  EQU  2                text
RBOLD  EQU  3                emphasis (bold)
RITAL  EQU  4                italic
RBOLIT EQU  5                bold italic
RSCRPT EQU  6                superscript and subscript
RDIM   EQU  7                dim: strikethrough, secondary text
RERROR EQU  8                error
RSELBG EQU  9                selection background
RSELFG EQU  10               selection foreground
RBARBG EQU  11               status bar background
RBARFG EQU  12               status bar foreground
RRULER EQU  13               ruler, rules, frames
RMARK  EQU  14               markers, labels
RACCNT EQU  15               accent: the cursor

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Video modes (video.a99: VMODE) and the VRAM map they share
*
* VMODE R0 = the base mode, R1 = options (added together):
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
VMT80  EQU  0                Text 80: 80 columns, 6 x 8 glyphs (the boot mode)
VMT40  EQU  1                Text 40: 40 columns, 6 x 8 glyphs
VMGM1  EQU  2                Graphics I: 32 columns, 8 x 8 tiles, 256 x 192
VMGM2  EQU  3                Graphics II (bitmap): 256 x 192, colour per 8 pixels
VMMC   EQU  4                Multicolor: 64 x 48 blocks
VO30   EQU  >0001            30 rows instead of 24 (every mode)
VOPOS  EQU  >0002            a colour attribute per screen position (Text 80/40; Graphics I with ECM)
VOTE1  EQU  >0004            enhanced colour mode 1 for tiles: 1 plane, 2 colours of a palette pair
VOTE2  EQU  >0008            ... mode 2: 2 planes, 4 colours
VOTE3  EQU  >000C            ... mode 3: 3 planes, 8 colours   (Text 80/40 and Graphics I only)
VOSE1  EQU  >0010            enhanced colour mode 1 for sprites
VOSE2  EQU  >0020            ... mode 2
VOSE3  EQU  >0030            ... mode 3
VOSPR  EQU  >0040            sprites on (VR51 = 32); off leaves them stopped
VOS16  EQU  >0080            sprites 16 x 16 (else 8 x 8)
VOSMG  EQU  >0100            sprites magnified x2
VMDEF  EQU  VO30+VOPOS       the boot mode: Text 80, 30 rows, attribute per position

* The text routines' geometry: set by VMODE
COLS   EQU  80               (the boot mode's columns: use TXCOLS in code that must follow the mode)
ROWS   EQU  30

* VRAM.  Every mode but Graphics II uses this one map, laid out so that the
* enhanced colour modes (which add pattern planes at +>800 and sprite planes at
* +>200) fit beside the sprites and the file service.
NAMTAB EQU  >0000            name table (Graphics II: NAMGM2)
GPUMB  EQU  >0960            the GPU's mailbox (8 bytes: a DMA's settings)
GPUAD  EQU  >0970            the GPU's program (about 26 bytes)
GM1CT  EQU  >09C0            Graphics I's colour table (32 bytes; with ECM or VOPOS ATRTAB is used)
ATRTAB EQU  >0A00            attribute table (2,400 bytes at 80 x 30)
SATAB  EQU  >1400            sprite attribute table (32 sprites x 4 bytes; links at +>80)
PATTAB EQU  >1800            pattern table, plane 0 (2,048 bytes); plane 1 at +>800, plane 2 at +>1000
SPTAB  EQU  >3000            sprite patterns, plane 0 (+>200 per plane: 64 patterns of 8 x 8)
* Graphics II (the standard bitmap map)
GM2PT  EQU  >0000            patterns (6,144 bytes)
NAMGM2 EQU  >1800            names (768 or 960 bytes)
GM2SAT EQU  >1C00            sprite attribute table
GM2CT  EQU  >2000            colours (6,144 bytes)
GM2SPT EQU  >3800            sprite patterns (these overlap the disk controller's area >37D8-3FFF)
* free for the application (Text 80, 30 rows): >09E0-09FF, >1500-17FF, and, when no
* sprites / ECM planes are used, >2000-35FF in pieces: see docs/VIDEO-MODES.md
* >3600-36FF the file record buffer, >3700-37D7 the PAB, >37D8-3FFF the
* disk controller's own (nothing else may touch it)

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* F18A registers, for VREG (the number written to a register is the
* byte in the high half of R1)
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
VR_MODE0 EQU  0              M3 (bit 1: bitmap), M4 (bit 2: with text, Text 80)
VR_MODE1 EQU  1              16K, display, interrupts, M1 (text), M2 (multicolor), sprite size, magnify
VR_NAME  EQU  2              name table, 1K units
VR_COLOR EQU  3              colour table, 64-byte units
VR_PATT  EQU  4              pattern table, 2K units
VR_SAT   EQU  5              sprite attribute table, 128-byte units
VR_SPT   EQU  6              sprite pattern table, 2K units
VR_BACK  EQU  7              text colours (fg/bg) and the backdrop
VR_NAME2 EQU  10             tile layer 2 name table, 1K units
VR_COLR2 EQU  11             tile layer 2 colour table, 64-byte units
VR_STAT  EQU  15             status register select
VR_HINT  EQU  19             horizontal interrupt line (0 = off)
VR_PALSL EQU  24             palette select: sprites bits 5-4, tile layer 2 bits 3-2, tile layer 1 bits 1-0
VR_HS2   EQU  25             tile layer 2 horizontal scroll
VR_VS2   EQU  26             tile layer 2 vertical scroll
VR_HS1   EQU  27             tile layer 1 horizontal scroll
VR_VS1   EQU  28             tile layer 1 vertical scroll
VR_PAGE  EQU  29             page sizes of the two layers; plane offsets of tiles (bits 3-2) and sprites (7-6)
VR_SPRMX EQU  30             sprites per scan line (0 = the jumper's choice)
VR_BMPCT EQU  31             bitmap layer control
VR_BMPAD EQU  32             bitmap layer base, 64-byte units
VR_BMPX  EQU  33             bitmap layer x
VR_BMPY  EQU  34             bitmap layer y
VR_BMPW  EQU  35             bitmap layer width (0 = 256)
VR_BMPH  EQU  36             bitmap layer height
VR_PALCT EQU  47             palette data port control
VR_INCR  EQU  48             VRAM address increment (signed)
VR_ECM   EQU  49             TL2 on (bit 7), 30 rows (6), tile ECM (5-4), real Y (3), linking (2), sprite ECM (1-0)
VR_FLAGS EQU  50             GPU triggers, tile layer 1 off (4), report max sprites (3), scan lines (2), position attributes (1), TL2 priority (0)
VR_STOP  EQU  51             stop sprite: sprites at this index and above are not drawn (32 = all drawn)
VR_GPUHI EQU  54             GPU start address, high byte
VR_GPULO EQU  55             ... low byte (writing it starts the GPU)
VR_GPUCT EQU  56             GPU control
VR_LOCK  EQU  57             unlock sequence

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* File service (files.a99)
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
FPABV  EQU  >3700            the PAB in VRAM
FBUFV  EQU  >3600            the record buffer in VRAM (up to 255 bytes)
* open flags (the PAB's flag byte, high byte of R0): add one from each group
FDISP  EQU  >0000            DISPLAY records
FINTL  EQU  >0800            INTERNAL records
FFIXED EQU  >0000            FIXED-length records
FVAR   EQU  >1000            VARIABLE-length records
FUPDT  EQU  >0000            mode: update
FOUT   EQU  >0200            mode: output (creates the file)
FIN    EQU  >0400            mode: input
FAPP   EQU  >0600            mode: append
* results (R0): 0 = done; 1-7 = the PAB's error code; >FF = no such device
FEEOF  EQU  5                reading past the end of the file
FENOFI EQU  7                a file error (the stand-in disk's answer for a missing input file;
*                            TI's own controller answers 2 there, and 7 for a close after end of file)
